# Tangerine Compiler — Proof-Carrying Patch (audit §37)
# Self-hosting compiler written in Tangerine
#
# proof_patch.tg assembles ONE audit §37 ProofCarryingPatch report from the
# §31 SemanticDelta (semantic_delta.tg) / §36 ChangeBudget (change_budget.tg)
# machinery: the report bundles the delta itself with the four
# compiler-verified stage flags, the delta's domain row lists (§31 records),
# the §36-rule unsafe-marker rows, the abi / public-api deltas, two
# extensible surfaces (affected tests, uncovered new paths) and the budget
# envelope's violation list. The module reads ONLY SemanticDelta and
# ChangeBudget records (never AST / typed / MIR state) and mirrors the
# semantic_delta.tg / change_budget.tg / repair_candidates.tg idioms.
#
# CLAIM DISCIPLINE — what "proven" means here. The audit is explicit: "Do
# not call arbitrary application behavior 'proven' — this covers only what
# the compiler/verifiers actually established." A ProofCarryingPatch carries
# (1) a semantic_delta (what the §31 diff recorded between two snapshots),
# (2) a budget_result (what the §36 envelope check decided) and (3) the
# compiler-verified flags parses / resolves / types / ownership — and
# nothing else is ever claimed "proven". In particular:
#   - The four flags reflect the compiler PIPELINE stages only: parses = the
#     source parsed; resolves = name resolution succeeded; types = type
#     checking succeeded; ownership = the access/ownership convention checks
#     succeeded — each for the programs the patch spans. The flags are never
#     inferred here: proof_patch_of_delta cannot see a pipeline and leaves
#     every flag false (an unset flag is an unproven property, never a
#     denied one). A flag becomes true only when the caller that RAN the
#     stage sets it. Repair machinery (repair_candidates.tg) is advisory
#     intent and is not part of the record: a patch reports what the
#     compiler established, never what a repair suggests.
#   - The row lists and deltas report what the §31/§36 machinery established
#     (row texts of the snapshot domain). No row is invented: the flat lists
#     are projections of existing delta rows, and a surface the delta cannot
#     see stays empty (documented gaps below).
#   - Application behavior (what the patched program DOES at run time) is
#     never a patch's claim. A verified budget says the change fits its
#     allowance envelope; it does not say the change is behaviorally
#     correct.
#
# RECORD-NAME MAPPING (the audit's names onto the §31 records that exist —
# the §29 adaptation discipline, never invented look-alikes):
#   - The audit's EffectDelta / CapabilityDelta rows are semantic_delta.tg's
#     EdgeDelta rows — ONE record covers both tables (key/kind/from/to; an
#     effect row's §11 dirty-word payload rides `to`). effect_delta /
#     capability_delta therefore hold Vec[EdgeDelta] with the §31 kinds
#     intact (a patch keeps each row's created/removed/modified state).
#   - ContractDelta / ApiDelta / AbiDelta / SemanticDelta exist under the
#     audit's names and are carried as-is.
#   - unsafe_delta holds TEXT rows (the audit's marker rows), not records:
#     the §31 record set has no unsafe row record (documented in
#     semantic_delta.tg — the unsafe_hash domain has no delta rows).
#   - affected_tests / uncovered_new_paths are Vec[String] extension
#     surfaces the audit names but SemanticDelta carries no test or coverage
#     rows for (tests/coverage/unsafe_hash carry no rows today — documented
#     in semantic_delta.tg): they stay empty here, and a consumer holding
#     the semantic-index test relationships (semantic_index.tg) or the
#     coverage machinery fills them. The module never synthesizes a test or
#     a path name from an affected key.
#
# FLAT-LIST DERIVATION (proof_patch_of_delta, over ONE delta + ONE budget —
# the module doc's numbered checks):
#   1. contract_delta := the delta's contract rows (§31 ContractDelta).
#   2. effect_delta := the delta's effect rows (§31 EdgeDelta; key + §11
#      dirty-word payload).
#   3. capability_delta := the delta's capability rows (§31 EdgeDelta; the
#      "cap<id>" part rides `to`).
#   4. unsafe_delta := the rows of the §36 EXACT COUNTING RULE
#      (change_budget.tg check 7): the CREATED ownership rows plus the
#      CREATED effect rows whose payload dirty word carries the §11 unsafe
#      class (bit 64), rendered in the change_budget marker vocabulary
#      minus the "budget:" prefix — "<fn-key> ownership-<convention>" /
#      "<fn-key> effect-unsafe", ownership rows first (check 7's listing
#      order), each group in the delta's own sorted row order.
#   5. abi_delta / public_api_delta := the delta's (hash-changed flag +
#      symmetric-difference rows).
#   6. budget_result := budget_verify(budget, delta) — the "budget:"
#      violation lines; empty = the delta verifies within the budget
#      envelope. That is the ONLY "budget verified" claim the patch makes.
#   7. The flags stay false and affected_tests / uncovered_new_paths stay
#      empty (see CLAIM DISCIPLINE / the mapping gaps above).
#
# EMPTY-DEFAULT: proof_patch_new() is the claim-nothing patch — the empty
# §31 delta (which verifies against ANY budget: an empty delta carries no
# keys, no rows and no counts), every flag false, every list empty. All Vec
# fields are defaulted so a patch can be assembled incrementally: rows push
# onto the lists and flags are set as the pipeline stages establish them.
# Re-running proof_patch_of_delta over the patch's semantic_delta re-derives
# the report lists from a delta + budget.
#
# ANCHORS:
#   ProofCarryingPatch        — the §37 protocol record (the audit's struct,
#                               field for field).
#   proof_patch_new           — the empty / claim-nothing patch.
#   proof_patch_of_delta      — the assembly convenience (delta + budget ->
#                               report; the derivation steps 1-7 above).
#   proof_patch_proven_fields — the true-flag name list (the claim surface:
#                               parses / resolves / types / ownership only,
#                               fixed pipeline order).
#   unsafe markers            — "<fn-key> ownership-<convention>" /
#                               "<fn-key> effect-unsafe" row texts (the §36
#                               counting rule's rows).

use tg_compiler::semantic_delta::{
  SemanticDelta, DeltaKind, ContractDelta, EdgeDelta, ApiDelta, AbiDelta, semantic_delta_empty
}
use tg_compiler::change_budget::{ChangeBudget, budget_verify}

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# The §37 protocol record
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

# ONE §37 report: the §31 delta carried whole, the compiler-verified stage
# flags, the delta's domain row lists, the abi / public-api deltas, the two
# extensible surfaces and the §36 budget verdict. Every Vec field is
# defaulted so the patch can be assembled incrementally (see the module
# doc); semantic_delta / abi_delta / public_api_delta have no default and
# must be named on construction (proof_patch_new supplies the empty forms).
struct ProofCarryingPatch
  # The §31 semantic delta itself — the diff this patch reports.
  semantic_delta: SemanticDelta
  # The compiler-verified stage flags (pipeline stages only — see CLAIM
  # DISCIPLINE; false = unproven, never a denial).
  parses: Bool = false
  resolves: Bool = false
  types: Bool = false
  ownership: Bool = false
  # The delta's contract rows (§31 ContractDelta; kinds intact).
  contract_delta: Vec[ContractDelta] = Vec::new()
  # The delta's effect rows (§31 EdgeDelta — the audit's EffectDelta).
  effect_delta: Vec[EdgeDelta] = Vec::new()
  # The delta's capability rows (§31 EdgeDelta — the audit's
  # CapabilityDelta).
  capability_delta: Vec[EdgeDelta] = Vec::new()
  # The §36-rule unsafe-marker rows ("<key> ownership-<convention>" /
  # "<key> effect-unsafe" — the module doc's derivation step 4).
  unsafe_delta: Vec[String] = Vec::new()
  # The ABI surface delta (changed flag + symmetric-difference rows).
  abi_delta: AbiDelta
  # The public-API surface delta.
  public_api_delta: ApiDelta
  # The tests the patch affects — an extension surface (no §31 test rows
  # today; a consumer holding the index/test relationships fills it).
  affected_tests: Vec[String] = Vec::new()
  # The newly uncovered paths — an extension surface (no §31 coverage rows
  # today; a consumer holding the coverage machinery fills it).
  uncovered_new_paths: Vec[String] = Vec::new()
  # The §36 envelope verdict: budget_verify(budget, delta)'s output lines
  # (empty = the delta verifies within the budget).
  budget_result: Vec[String] = Vec::new()
end

# The empty / claim-nothing patch: the empty §31 delta (which verifies
# against ANY budget), every stage flag false, no rows, no violations.
# proof_patch_proven_fields over it is empty — nothing is claimed.
pub def proof_patch_new() -> ProofCarryingPatch
  ProofCarryingPatch {
    semantic_delta: semantic_delta_empty(),
    parses: false,
    resolves: false,
    types: false,
    ownership: false,
    abi_delta: AbiDelta {
      abi_changed: false
    },
    public_api_delta: ApiDelta {
      public_api_changed: false
    }
  }
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# Kind / word primitives (the change_budget.tg byte-text idioms)
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

# Whether a §31 DeltaKind is Created — the row state the §36 counting rule
# counts (the created/removed/modified discrimination of every §31 row).
def proof_kind_created(k: DeltaKind) -> Bool
  match k
  when DeltaKind::Created then true
  when DeltaKind::Removed then false
  when DeltaKind::Modified then false
  end
end

# The integer value of the leading ASCII-digit run of a text (0 when the
# text does not start with a digit). The §11 dirty word of an effect row is
# the leading run of its "<word>;<cap-ids>" payload text. Byte-parse idiom
# mirrors change_budget.tg's budget_int_prefix.
def proof_int_prefix(s: String) -> Int
  var value = 0
  var i = 0
  while i < s.len() do
    let bs = s.slice(i, i + 1).bytes()
    if bs.len() == 0 then
      break
    end
    let b = bs[0] as Int
    if b >= 48 && b <= 57 then
      value = value * 10 + (b - 48)
      i = i + 1
    else
      break
    end
  end
  value
end

# Whether a §11 dirty word carries the class of the given bit (the word is
# the sum of its class bits: allocate=1, block=2, io=4, spawn=8, sleep=16,
# panic=32, unsafe=64 — mirror of change_budget.tg's budget_word_has_class;
# the §36 rule's unsafe class is bit 64).
def proof_word_has_class(word: Int, bit: Int) -> Bool
  (word / bit) % 2 == 1
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# The §36-rule unsafe-marker rows
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# The rows of change_budget.tg's EXACT COUNTING RULE (check 7): the CREATED
# ownership rows plus the CREATED effect rows whose payload dirty word
# carries the §11 unsafe class (bit 64) — rendered in the change_budget
# marker vocabulary without the "budget:" prefix, ownership rows first (its
# listing order), each group in the delta's own (sorted) row order.
def proof_unsafe_rows(delta: SemanticDelta) -> Vec[String]
  var out: Vec[String] = Vec::new()
  for row in delta.ownership_delta do
    if proof_kind_created(row.kind) then
      out.push(row.key.clone() + " ownership-" + row.convention.clone())
    end
  end
  for row in delta.effect_delta do
    if proof_kind_created(row.kind) then
      let eword = proof_int_prefix(row.to)
      if proof_word_has_class(eword, 64) then
        out.push(row.key.clone() + " effect-unsafe")
      end
    end
  end
  out
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# THE ASSEMBLY — ONE ProofCarryingPatch from ONE delta + ONE budget
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# Derives the report's flat lists from the §31 delta (the module doc's
# steps 1-5), runs the §36 envelope (step 6: budget_result :=
# budget_verify(budget, delta) — empty = the delta verifies within the
# budget) and leaves the stage flags false / extension surfaces empty (step
# 7): nothing a delta alone carries can prove that a pipeline stage ran, so
# this function never claims one (see CLAIM DISCIPLINE).
pub def proof_patch_of_delta(budget: ChangeBudget, delta: SemanticDelta) -> ProofCarryingPatch
  let violations = budget_verify(&budget, &delta)
  let contract_rows = delta.contract_delta.clone()
  let effect_rows = delta.effect_delta.clone()
  let capability_rows = delta.capability_delta.clone()
  let unsafe_rows = proof_unsafe_rows(&delta)
  let api_changed = delta.public_api_delta.public_api_changed
  let api_rows = delta.public_api_delta.public_api_rows.clone()
  let abi_changed = delta.abi_delta.abi_changed
  let abi_rows = delta.abi_delta.abi_rows.clone()
  ProofCarryingPatch {
    semantic_delta: delta,
    parses: false,
    resolves: false,
    types: false,
    ownership: false,
    contract_delta: contract_rows,
    effect_delta: effect_rows,
    capability_delta: capability_rows,
    unsafe_delta: unsafe_rows,
    abi_delta: AbiDelta {
      abi_changed: abi_changed,
      abi_rows: abi_rows
    },
    public_api_delta: ApiDelta {
      public_api_changed: api_changed,
      public_api_rows: api_rows
    }
  }
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# The claim surface — the proven-field name list
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# The names of the stage flags that are TRUE, in the fixed pipeline order
# (parses, resolves, types, ownership). These four reflect the compiler
# pipeline stages ONLY (see the module doc); an empty list means no stage
# property is claimed — the fail-closed claim surface, never a denial of a
# property the pipeline actually established.
pub def proof_patch_proven_fields(patch: ProofCarryingPatch) -> Vec[String]
  var out: Vec[String] = Vec::new()
  if patch.parses then
    out.push("parses")
  end
  if patch.resolves then
    out.push("resolves")
  end
  if patch.types then
    out.push("types")
  end
  if patch.ownership then
    out.push("ownership")
  end
  out
end
